messages:   20%|#9        | 117/600 [00:56<05:00,  1.60it/s][["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nThose who claim that governments should not continue to devote resources to space exploration are wrong. Although most people' s lives are relatively unaffected by the direct consequences of space exploration, many modern technologies that have a tremendous impact on daily life -- e. g. , fiber optics, computers, and lasers -- are unexpected consequences of it. Society might have missed the benefits of these technologies if governments had not devoted resources to space exploration.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which one of the following most accurately expresses the principle underlying the argument above?\n# Options:\nA) Governments should continue to support those projects that have, in the past, produced unintended benefits.\nB) Governments should not be prevented from allocating resources to projects whose intended consequences do not directly benefit most people.\nC) One can never underestimate the beneficial consequences of government support of ambitious technological undertakings.\nD) The less practical the goal of a government- supported project, the more unexpected the consequences of that project.", '<think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, we need to identify the principle underlying the argument.The argument states that despite space exploration directly benefiting most people, it has not led to any unexpected technological advancements that have had a significant impact on daily life. This implies that the principle underlying the argument is that governments should be prevented from allocating resources to ambitious technological undertakings, even if the intended consequences directly benefit most people. This is because these projects may not have unexpected benefits that can have a significant impact on society.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
