messages:   21%|##        | 125/600 [01:03<08:34,  1.08s/it][['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nAn expert made statistics on the life expectancy of the late Nobel laureates in economics and found that their average life expectancy was 85 years old, of which most were over 90 years old, many were over 100 years old, and the youngest died at the age of 74 years. As a result, the expert believes that winning the Nobel Prize in economics can make people live longer.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following, if true, would most weaken the opinions of the above-mentioned experts?\n# Options:\nA) The nobel prize in economics is only awarded to living scholars. this awarding rule is extremely beneficial to scholars who have lived a long life\nB) Winning the nobel prize can bring great physical and mental pleasure to fame, and a happy physical and mental state can prolong life\nC) Keynes, the father of macroeconomics, passed away at the age of 63. it is a pity that he did not win the nobel prize in economics\nD) Scholars who have won the nobel prize in physics have a long life span, but none of them have won the nobel prize in economics', "<think>.\nTo determine which option most weakens the expert's opinion, we need to consider how each option affects the relationship between winning the Nobel Prize in economics and increased life expectancy.Let's analyze each option step by step:.\n\nA) The Nobel Prize in Economics is only awarded to living scholars.This rule is extremely beneficial to scholars who have lived a long life.\n- This option suggests that the Nobel Prize in Economics may not be directly related to increased life expectancy because it only awards living scholars.However, this doesn't necessarily weaken the expert's opinion, as it doesn't directly address the correlation between the prize and life expectancy.\n\nB) Winning the Nobel Prize can bring great physical and mental pleasure to fame, and a happy physical and mental state can prolong life.\n- This option directly addresses the potential mechanism by which winning the Nobel Prize might increase life expectancy.If true, it would weaken the expert's opinion by suggesting that the increased life expectancy is due to the happiness and pleasure associated with winning the prize, rather than the prize itself.\n\nC) Keynes, the father of macroeconomics, passed away at the age of 63.It is a pity that he did not win the Nobel Prize in Economics.Winning the Nobel Prize in Physics is not correlated with a long life span, and many winners of the Nobel Prize in Economics have also lived to a ripe old age.. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
