tside the home is 25 percent higher than national guidelines recommend.\nThis option suggests that the dinners prepared by adults who do not work outside the home may be less healthy due to higher fat content.If this is true, it could explain why the dinners prepared by these adults are less nutritious, even though the dinners prepared by adults who work outside the home do not differ significantly in nutritional value.This option helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy.\n\nB) Adults who work outside the home eat dinner at home 20 percent less often than do adults who do not work outside the home.\nThis option suggests that adults who work outside the home eat dinner at home less frequently than those who do not work outside the home.If this is true, it could explain why the dinners prepared by adults who work outside the home do not differ significantly in nutritional value, variety of menus, or number of courses.This option helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy.\n\nC) Adults who do not work outside the home tend to prepare breakfast more often than adults who work outside the home.\nBoth options A and B do not help to resolve the apparent discrepancy.\n. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
