messages:   88%|########7 | 525/600 [05:02<00:44,  1.68it/s][['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nWittgenstein was a student of Moore, a famous philosopher at Cambridge University. One day, philosopher Russell asked Moore, who is your best student? Moore said without hesitation: Wittgenstein. why? because among all the students, only he always showed a blank look when listening to the class, and there were always endless questions. later, Wittgenstein is more famous than Russell. Someone asked, why did Russell fall behind? Wittgenstein said: he has no problem.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, this paragraph is intended to illustrate that:\n# Options:\nA) Doubting and questioning are always the secret of success\nB) Curiosity is the internal driving force of research\nC) Appreciation is an important factor in success\nD) Finding problems is the premise of continuous progress', "<think>.\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, we need to analyze the given context and question step by step.Let's break it down:.\n\n1.**Context Analysis:**.\n   - Wittgenstein was a student of Moore.\n   - Moore was a famous philosopher at Cambridge University.\n   - Russell asked Moore who his best student was.\n   - Moore replied that Wittgenstein was his best student.\n   - Wittgenstein was more famous than Russell.\n   - Someone asked Wittgenstein why he was more famous than Russell.\n   - Wittgenstein said that Russell fell behind because he had no problem.\n\n2.**Question Analysis:**.\n   - The question asks us to identify the main point illustrated by the paragraph.\n\n3.**Evaluate Each Option:**.\n   - **Option A: Doubting and questioning are always the secret of success.**.\n     - This option suggests that doubting and questioning are essential for success.While Wittgenstein's questioning might be seen as a positive trait, the context doesn't explicitly state that doubting and questioning are the sole secret to success.\n   - **Option B: Curiosity is the internal driving force of research.**.\n     - This option suggests that curiosity drives research.The context explicitly states that appreciation is the main factor in Wittgenstein's success.. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
